A||N||6||lews84||Oil leaks around valve cover - help!||||5
Z||000000||lews84||09-13-1999||09:12 PM||||Even after all the call backs (except the most recent one for brakes)I still have oil all over the valve cover.<P>Has anyone found a solution to the oil leaks other than degreasing the engine once a month?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-14-1999||09:01 AM||||replace the PCV gromits, and oil cap. Replace the valve cover gasket. If the gasket was replaced and it's still leaking there, then the cover has gotten bent up. While posible to flatten the flange back out, it's easier for most people to find a cover in better shape.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan||09-14-1999||12:18 PM|||| Got what you need to know to take good care of leaky valve covers for the life of your engine.Guaranteed for the life of your engine for only $39.95. Remove your valve cover and completely remove all gasket material on both surfaces.While your'e at it,<BR>go ahead and clean your valve cover in mineral spirits ,dry and sand the gasket surface with 150 grit sandpaper.Also sand all surface impurfections down to bare metal <BR>if necessary and finish over all the outside of the cover with 350 grit.Lay the valve cover back on the head,and start three bolts to hold it in place.Use a hammer and a brass punch or round stock to flatten the lip.Check for straightness by grasping the cover in the middle and try to rock it back and forth while pressing down. This will tell you where on the lip the high spot must be recalibrated,I mean restraightened.Remove valve cover,clean with brake cleaner and wipe off.Spray paint with favorite brand of black gloss enamel spray paint, I use Ace Hardwares brand myself. Only paint outside of valve cover.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan||09-14-1999||12:35 PM||||The inside gasket surface mest remain clean and bare metal. Let dry for 24 hours. You can also sand and clean the air filter and egr valve  bolts and paint. Sand and clean gasket surface on head.Take any RTV gasket sealer and apply a 1/4" round bead all around the valve cover gasket surface and lay on the head. Start all bolts then in a cross torque method go over the bolts 3 times.Using rtv silicone it requires bare metal surfaces, paint won't stick to it and it won't stick as well to paint.The valve cover doesn't need to be tightened all the way down, and if you use a nut driver you can feel the torque on the bolts better.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dan||09-14-1999||12:46 PM||||Let RTV dry for 24 hours.Add new grommets and pcv valve and you have a leak free Fiero<BR>and very attractive under the hood,plus you saved $39.95.The Rtv will last leak free until you want to remove it again.The first time I did one,mine, it leaked right off the bat and I had to remove it, clean it, and do it again. Just make sure your bead is a 1/4" <BR>round bead evenly applied and I'll bet you could get it right the first time.No need to tighten all the way, only enough for the rtv to seal all the way around. Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||09-15-1999||02:08 PM||||Great advice Dan. Most of the time, the valve cover bolts pull the metal down a little right around the bolt holes. Follow Dan's procedure to get a flat surface again and also you might consider putting on those valve cover hold down tabs (they are sort of elongated washers like things that you often see on small block Chevys). This spreads the bolt tension out over a larger area which helps sealing and prevents the bolts from warping the valve cover as bad.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan||09-21-1999||08:58 PM|||| Won't need the extra pieces. If you make a good spread with a 1/4" bead, you end up using the rtv for an adhesive and actually glue the parts together.||||reg||7
